## Why This Is Asked
This question tests real-world integration of dynamic forms, streaming data, and memoization under per-project scope.

## Key Concepts
- **useSyncExternalStore** for streaming form data
- **Memoized selectors** with composite keys (projectId, formId, step)
- **Dynamic forms** with field-level memoization
- **react-window** virtualization for long field lists
- **Optimistic updates** with per-field rollback on server rejection
- Proper **cleanup** on project switch and stream unsubscription

## Code Example
```typescript
function useFormStream(projectId: string, formId: string, step: number) {
  const store = useRef(getPerProjectStore(projectId));
  const selector = useMemo(
    () => createVisibleFieldsSelector(projectId, formId, step),
    [projectId, formId, step]
  );

  return useSyncExternalStore(
    store.current.subscribe,
    () => selector(store.current.getState()),
    () => selector(store.current.getServerState())
  );
}

const FieldRow = React.memo(({ field, value, onChange }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <FieldInput field={field} value={value} onChange={onChange} />
    </div>
  );
});

function FormStep({ projectId, formId, step }) {
  const fields = useFormStream(projectId, formId, step);

  return (
    <FixedSizeList height={600} itemData={fields}>
      {({ index, style }) => (
        <FieldRow 
          field={fields[index]} 
          style={style}
        />
      )}
    </FixedSizeList>
  );
}
```

## Implementation Notes
1. The selector recomputes only when (projectId, formId, step) changes
2. FieldRow memoization prevents re-renders when unrelated fields update
3. react-window renders only visible rows, reducing DOM nodes
4. On server rejection, restore the previous field value from a snapshot taken before the optimistic update
5. Unsubscribe from the stream and clear selectors in a cleanup effect when projectId changes

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a small data-layer primitive using closures, promises, and batching for performance.

## Key Concepts
- Closures and per-key caches
- Promise queuing and batch network calls
- TTL-based eviction and memory safety
- Error handling and cancellation tokens

## Code Example
```javascript
function createBatchingCache(fetcher, { batchDelay = 100, ttl = 5000 } = {}) {
  const cache = new Map(); // key -> { value, expiresAt }
  const inFlight = new Map(); // key -> { promise, resolve, reject }
  let batch = [];
  let timer = null;

  async function flush() {
    const keys = batch;
    batch = [];
    const uniq = Array.from(new Set(keys));
    try {
      const results = await fetcher(uniq);
      uniq.forEach((k, i) => {
        const v = results[i];
        cache.set(k, { value: v, expiresAt: Date.now() + ttl });
        if (inFlight.has(k)) inFlight.get(k).resolve(v);
        inFlight.delete(k);
      });
    } catch (e) {
      uniq.forEach(k => inFlight.get(k)?.reject(e));
      uniq.forEach(k => inFlight.delete(k));
    }
  }

  return {
    get(key) {
      const c = cache.get(key);
      if (c && c.expiresAt > Date.now()) return Promise.resolve(c.value);
      if (inFlight.has(key)) return inFlight.get(key).promise;
      let resolve, reject;
      const p = new Promise((res, rej) => { resolve = res; reject = rej; });
      inFlight.set(key, { promise: p, resolve, reject });
      batch.push(key);
      if (!timer) timer = setTimeout(() => { timer = null; flush(); }, batchDelay);
      return p;
    },
    clear() {
      cache.clear();
      inFlight.clear();
      batch = [];
      if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);
      timer = null;
    }
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a streaming, per-tenant UI with time-based playback, memory controls, and precise cleanup when tenants switch. It exercises useSyncExternalStore, memoized selectors keyed by (tenantId, timeWindow), bounded ring buffers to cap memory, React.memo for EventRow components, playback controls (play/pause and seek), and tenant switch cleanup.

## Key Concepts
- useSyncExternalStore for external streams
- memoized selectors keyed by (tenantId, timeWindow)
- Bounded ring buffer to cap memory
- React.memo for EventRow components
- Playback controls: play/pause and seek
- Tenant switch cleanup

## Code Example
```javascript
// Stream wrapper using useSyncExternalStore
function useAuditStream(tenantId) {
  const storeRef = useRef(null);
  if (!storeRef.current) {
    storeRef.current = createAuditStore(tenantId);
  }
  return useSyncExternalStore(
    storeRef.current.subscribe,
    storeRef.current.getSnapshot,
    storeRef.current.getServerSnapshot
  );
}

// Memoized selector keyed by (tenantId, timeWindow)
const selectBoundedEvents = memoize((tenantId, timeWindow) => {
  return (events) => {
    const cutoff = Date.now() - timeWindow;
    return events
      .filter(e => e.tenantId === tenantId && e.timestamp > cutoff)
      .slice(-120); // Bounded to 120 events
  };
});

// React.memo EventRow
const EventRow = memo(({ event }) => (
  <div>{event.action} - {event.timestamp}</div>
));

// Panel wiring
function AuditPanel({ tenantId }) {
  const events = useAuditStream(tenantId);
  const boundedEvents = useMemo(
    () => selectBoundedEvents(tenantId, 120000),
    [tenantId]
  )(events);

  return (
    <div>
      {boundedEvents.map(e => <EventRow key={e.id} event={e} />)}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests integration of fetch, WebSocket, Service Worker, and offline persistence in a realistic UI while focusing on DOM efficiency.

## Key Concepts

- Ordered patch application with per-patch IDs and timestamps
- Service Worker caching for REST data and assets with stale-while-revalidate
- WebSocket reconnect strategy and backoff
- IndexedDB as an offline queue with idempotent replay and conflict checks

## Code Example

```javascript
function applyPatches(root, patches){
  // patches: [{id, type, selector, value, ts}]
  patches.sort((a,b)=> a.id - b.id);
  for(const p of patches){
    // minimal DOM update logic depending on type
    const el = root.querySelector(p.selector);
    if(!el) continue;
    switch(p.type){
      case 'update': el.textContent = p.value; break;
      case 'move': el.style.transform = `translate(${p.value.x}px, ${p.value.y}px)`; break;
    }
  }
}
```

